lass="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td></tr><tr><th class="rn">18</th><th class="rl">Cherry shrimp (neocaridina)</th><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m n">✗</td></tr><tr><th class="rn">19</th><th class="rl">Amano shrimp</th><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m n">✗</td></tr><tr><th class="rn">20</th><th class="rl">Snails (nerite, mystery)</th><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m y">✓</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m n">✗</td></tr><tr><th class="rn">21</th><th class="rl">Freshwater crabs</th><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m c">~</td><td class="m n">✗</td></tr><tr><th class="rn">22</th><th class="rl">Crayfish / ''lobsters''</th><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m n">✗</td><td class="m c">~</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="vet-tip"><h4>&#128031; Setting up your first tank?</h4><p>Compatibility only matters once the tank is safe to live in. Read our <a href="/blog/aquarium-nitrogen-cycle-tank-setup-guide">complete nitrogen cycle &amp; tank setup guide</a> first &mdash; it covers cycling step by step, plus eight tank sizes and what fits in each.</p></div><h2>⚠️ The Pairings People Get Wrong</h2>
<p>Eight combinations account for most of the compatibility problems we see. If you''re planning any of these, read this bit twice.</p>

<div class="vet-warning">
  <h4>⚠️ Betta + male guppies — the classic mistake</h4>
  <p>Bright colours and long flowing fins are exactly what a male betta is programmed to attack — a fancy guppy looks like a rival betta. It rarely ends well. Female guppies in a large planted tank sometimes work, but it''s a gamble on an individual fish''s temperament.</p>
</div>

<div class="vet-warning">
  <h4>⚠️ Angelfish + neon tetras — fine, until it isn''t</h4>
  <p>The most-repeated pairing in the hobby, and it works beautifully for about eight months. Then the angelfish reaches adult size and neons become correctly-sized food. Angels are cichlids; if a fish fits in the mouth, it eventually goes in. Pair adult angels with larger tetras — black skirt, lemon, rummy-nose — instead.</p>
</div>

<div class="vet-warning">
  <h4>⚠️ Fin-nippers + anything with long fins</h4>
  <p>Tiger barbs, serpae tetras and black skirt tetras nip. It''s not aggression exactly — it''s what they do — and the targets are bettas, gouramis, angelfish, guppies and fancy-finned anything. Keeping nippers in groups of <strong>eight or more</strong> genuinely helps, because they take it out on each other instead, but never house them with trailing fins.</p>
</div>

<div class="vet-warning">
  <h4>⚠️ Snail-eating loaches + your snails</h4>
  <p>Clown loaches, yoyo loaches and skunk loaches are sold as the natural solution to a pest snail problem. They are — and they don''t stop at pest snails. Your £8 nerites and mystery snails are on the same menu. Clown loaches also reach a foot long and need a 75-gallon-plus tank, which the shop won''t mention.</p>
</div>

<div class="vet-warning">
  <h4>⚠️ Goldfish + tropical fish</h4>
  <p>Two separate problems. First temperature: goldfish are coolwater fish that do best around 65–72°F, while most tropicals want 76–80°F — one of them is always uncomfortable. Second waste: goldfish produce enormous amounts of it, which overwhelms the filtration a community tank runs on. And once a goldfish is grown, small tetras are snacks. Keep goldfish with goldfish.</p>
</div>

<div class="vet-warning">
  <h4>⚠️ Crayfish + literally anything</h4>
  <p>Sold as "freshwater lobsters" and marketed as community-safe. They aren''t. Crayfish hunt at night, catching sleeping fish off plants and the substrate, and they''ll clear a shrimp colony and a snail population in weeks. They''re genuinely interesting animals — in a tank of their own.</p>
</div>

<div class="vet-warning">
  <h4>⚠️ Anything + baby shrimp</h4>
  <p>Adult cherry shrimp survive alongside most peaceful community fish. <strong>Shrimplets do not.</strong> If you want a colony that actually breeds, you need either a shrimp-only tank or a heavily planted one with moss and hiding places — or you accept that your shrimp population will never grow.</p>
</div>

<div class="vet-warning">
  <h4>⚠️ "Freshwater" crabs that aren''t</h4>
  <p>Red claw crabs are the ones you''ll see most often, and they need <strong>brackish water and land to climb out onto</strong> — kept fully submerged in freshwater they slowly decline. Vampire crabs need a paludarium. Thai micro crabs are the genuine exception: fully aquatic, tiny, and completely peaceful. Check which one you''re actually buying before it goes in a community tank.</p>
</div>

<h2>🐟 Species Profiles: What Each One Actually Needs</h2>

<h3>Centrepiece &amp; personality fish</h3>

<div class="fish-card caution">
  <h3>1 · Betta (Siamese fighting fish)</h3>
  <div class="latin">Betta splendens</div>
  <div class="fish-spec"><span><b>Size:</b> 2.5–3"</span><span><b>Min tank:</b> 5 gal</span><span><b>Temp:</b> 76–82°F</span><span><b>Level:</b> Top/mid</span><span><b>Temperament:</b> Territorial</span></div>
  <p>Never house two males together — that part is famous. What''s less well known is how variable individuals are: some bettas ignore tank mates entirely, others attack everything. Always have a spare tank or a divider ready.</p>
  <p class="goes-with"><strong>Good with:</strong> corydoras, kuhli loaches, otocinclus, snails, harlequin rasbora, ember tetras (in 10 gal+).</p>
  <p class="avoid"><strong>Avoid:</strong> guppies, gouramis, other bettas, fin-nippers, anything with flowing fins.</p>
</div>

<div class="fish-card caution">
  <h3>13 · Angelfish</h3>
  <div class="latin">Pterophyllum scalare</div>
  <div class="fish-spec"><span><b>Size:</b> 6" tall, 8"+ with fins</span><span><b>Min tank:</b> 29 gal (40+ better)</span><span><b>Temp:</b> 78–84°F</span><span><b>Level:</b> Mid</span><span><b>Temperament:</b> Semi-aggressive cichlid</span></div>
  <p>Beautiful, intelligent and genuinely interactive — but they''re cichlids, and they grow tall, so they need height as well as footprint. A breeding pair will claim half the tank and defend it fiercely.</p>
  <p class="goes-with"><strong>Good with:</strong> corydoras, bristlenose plecos, larger tetras, rams, kuhli loaches.</p>
  <p class="avoid"><strong>Avoid:</strong> neon tetras (as adults), guppies, shrimp, fin-nippers, bettas.</p>
</div>

<div class="fish-card caution">
  <h3>12 · Gouramis (dwarf, honey, pearl)</h3>
  <div class="latin">Trichogaster / Trichopodus species</div>
  <div class="fish-spec"><span><b>Size:</b> 2–5"</span><span><b>Min tank:</b> 20 gal (pearl: 29+)</span><span><b>Temp:</b> 75–82°F</span><span><b>Level:</b> Top/mid</span><span><b>Temperament:</b> Peaceful but territorial</span></div>
  <p>Honey gouramis are the gentlest and the best beginner choice; dwarf gouramis are prettier but prone to a well-known iridovirus, so buy from a good source. Males of any species will squabble — keep one male per tank unless it''s large.</p>
  <p class="goes-with"><strong>Good with:</strong> tetras, rasboras, corydoras, livebearers, plecos, loaches.</p>
  <p class="avoid"><strong>Avoid:</strong> bettas, fin-nippers (they shred the long feelers), other male gouramis in small tanks.</p>
</div>

<div class="fish-card caution">
  <h3>14 · Rams &amp; apistogramma (dwarf cichlids)</h3>
  <div class="latin">Mikrogeophagus ramirezi / Apistogramma spp.</div>
  <div class="fish-spec"><span><b>Size:</b> 2–3"</span><span><b>Min tank:</b> 20 gal</span><span><b>Temp:</b> 78–85°F (German blue), 72–79°F (Bolivian)</span><span><b>Level:</b> Bottom/mid</span><span><b>Temperament:</b> Peaceful, territorial when breeding</span></div>
  <p>All the personality of a cichlid in a community-safe package. <strong>Bolivian rams are far hardier</strong> than German blue rams, which are widely captive-bred to fragility and want very warm, clean, soft water — a poor first cichlid despite being sold as one.</p>
  <p class="goes-with"><strong>Good with:</strong> tetras, rasboras, corydoras, plecos, angelfish, gouramis.</p>
  <p class="avoid"><strong>Avoid:</strong> African cichlids, large cichlids, other pairs of the same species in small tanks.</p>
</div>

<h3>Schooling fish — buy 6 minimum, 8–10 is better</h3>

<div class="fish-card peaceful">
  <h3>2 · Small tetras &amp; rasboras</h3>
  <div class="latin">Neon, cardinal, ember, rummy-nose · harlequin, chili rasbora</div>
  <div class="fish-spec"><span><b>Size:</b> 0.75–2"</span><span><b>Min tank:</b> 10–20 gal</span><span><b>Temp:</b> 72–80°F</span><span><b>Level:</b> Mid</span><span><b>Temperament:</b> Peaceful</span></div>
  <p>The backbone of most community tanks. A tight school of ten looks vastly better than three of four different species — resist the urge to mix. Neons and cardinals are lovely but genuinely sensitive to imperfect water, so put them in a tank that''s been running for a few months rather than a freshly cycled one.</p>
  <p class="goes-with"><strong>Good with:</strong> almost every peaceful community fish, corydoras, plecos, gouramis, rams.</p>
  <p class="avoid"><strong>Avoid:</strong> adult angelfish, African and large cichlids, goldfish.</p>
</div>

<div class="fish-card caution">
  <h3>3 · Fin-nippers (tiger barb, serpae tetra, black skirt)</h3>
  <div class="fish-spec"><span><b>Size:</b> 2–3"</span><span><b>Min tank:</b> 20 gal long</span><span><b>Temp:</b> 74–80°F</span><span><b>Level:</b> Mid</span><span><b>Temperament:</b> Boisterous, nippy</span></div>
  <p>Genuinely great fish in the right tank — active, colourful and hardy. The rule is simple: <strong>groups of 8+, and nothing with long fins.</strong> A small group of tiger barbs is far nippier than a large one, because the pecking order has nowhere to go.</p>
  <p class="goes-with"><strong>Good with:</strong> danios, other barbs, corydoras, plecos, loaches, fast robust fish.</p>
  <p class="avoid"><strong>Avoid:</strong> bettas, gouramis, angelfish, guppies, shrimp.</p>
</div>

<div class="fish-card peaceful">
  <h3>4 · Danios (zebra, pearl, celestial pearl)</h3>
  <div class="latin">Danio rerio and relatives</div>
  <div class="fish-spec"><span><b>Size:</b> 1–2.5"</span><span><b>Min tank:</b> 10–20 gal</span><span><b>Temp:</b> 64–77°F</span><span><b>Level:</b> Top</span><span><b>Temperament:</b> Peaceful but very active</span></div>
  <p>Among the hardiest fish sold and excellent for a newer tank. They''re fast and constantly busy, which can stress out slow, placid fish. Their tolerance for cooler water makes them one of the very few tropicals that <em>can</em> share a tank with goldfish — though the waste load still makes it a compromise.</p>
  <p class="goes-with"><strong>Good with:</strong> barbs, livebearers, corydoras, loaches, plecos.</p>
  <p class="avoid"><strong>Avoid:</strong> long-finned bettas, very shy or slow species.</p>
</div>

<h3>Livebearers — the ones that breed whether you want them to or not</h3>

<div class="fish-card peaceful">
  <h3>5 · Guppies &amp; Endler''s</h3>
  <div class="latin">Poecilia reticulata / Poecilia wingei</div>
  <div class="fish-spec"><span><b>Size:</b> 1–2.5"</span><span><b>Min tank:</b> 10 gal</span><span><b>Temp:</b> 74–82°F</span><span><b>Level:</b> Top/mid</span><span><b>Temperament:</b> Peaceful</span></div>
  <p>Cheerful, colourful and enthusiastically reproductive. One female arrives already pregnant and can store sperm for months, so a mixed group becomes a population. <strong>Keep all-male groups</strong> unless you want fry, and if you do keep females, aim for two or three per male so no single female gets harassed.</p>
  <p class="goes-with"><strong>Good with:</strong> tetras, rasboras, corydoras, otocinclus, plecos, other livebearers.</p>
  <p class="avoid"><strong>Avoid:</strong> bettas, fin-nippers, angelfish, any cichlid.</p>
</div>

<div class="fish-card peaceful">
  <h3>6 · Platies, mollies &amp; swordtails</h3>
  <div class="latin">Xiphophorus / Poecilia species</div>
  <div class="fish-spec"><span><b>Size:</b> 2–5"</span><span><b>Min tank:</b> 20 gal (mollies 29+)</span><span><b>Temp:</b> 72–82°F</span><span><b>Level:</b> Mid/top</span><span><b>Temperament:</b> Peaceful, males can chase</span></div>
  <p>Platies are among the best beginner fish there are — hardy, colourful and undemanding. <strong>Mollies are the fussy one</strong>: they want hard, mineral-rich water and often do poorly in soft water, which surprises people who bought them as easy. Swordtail males can be pushy, so give them length to swim.</p>
  <p class="goes-with"><strong>Good with:</strong> tetras, danios, corydoras, gouramis, plecos, other livebearers.</p>
  <p class="avoid"><strong>Avoid:</strong> aggressive cichlids, goldfish, fin-nippers (with fancy varieties).</p>
</div>
<h3>Bottom dwellers &amp; clean-up crew</h3>

<div class="fish-card peaceful">
  <h3>7 · Corydoras catfish</h3>
  <div class="latin">Corydoras species</div>
  <div class="fish-spec"><span><b>Size:</b> 1–3"</span><span><b>Min tank:</b> 10 gal (pygmy) / 20 gal</span><span><b>Temp:</b> 72–79°F</span><span><b>Level:</b> Bottom</span><span><b>Temperament:</b> Peaceful, social</span></div>
  <p>The most universally compatible fish in the hobby — the one row on our chart that''s green almost the whole way across. They''re shoaling animals, so <strong>six minimum</strong>; a lone cory is a stressed cory. Give them sand or smooth gravel, because sharp substrate wears down their barbels and causes infections.</p>
  <p><strong>Important:</strong> they are not a cleaning crew. Corydoras need their own sinking food — they will starve while politely hoovering up nothing in a tank where everything is eaten at the surface.</p>
  <p class="goes-with"><strong>Good with:</strong> essentially every peaceful community fish, plus angelfish, gouramis and rams.</p>
  <p class="avoid"><strong>Avoid:</strong> African and large cichlids, goldfish, crayfish.</p>
</div>

<div class="fish-card peaceful">
  <h3>8 · Bristlenose pleco</h3>
  <div class="latin">Ancistrus species</div>
  <div class="fish-spec"><span><b>Size:</b> 4–6"</span><span><b>Min tank:</b> 20–29 gal</span><span><b>Temp:</b> 73–82°F</span><span><b>Level:</b> Bottom</span><span><b>Temperament:</b> Peaceful, males territorial</span></div>
  <p><strong>This is the pleco to buy</strong> — the common pleco sold beside it reaches 18–24 inches. Bristlenose stay manageable, genuinely eat algae, and are tough enough to live alongside semi-aggressive fish. They need <strong>driftwood</strong>: the cellulose is a dietary requirement, not decoration. Supplement with algae wafers and courgette.</p>
  <p class="goes-with"><strong>Good with:</strong> nearly everything, including angelfish and (often) African cichlids.</p>
  <p class="avoid"><strong>Avoid:</strong> other male bristlenose in small tanks, goldfish, crayfish.</p>
</div>

<div class="fish-card caution">
  <h3>9 · Otocinclus</h3>
  <div class="latin">Otocinclus species</div>
  <div class="fish-spec"><span><b>Size:</b> 1.5–2"</span><span><b>Min tank:</b> 10 gal</span><span><b>Temp:</b> 72–79°F</span><span><b>Level:</b> Bottom/glass</span><span><b>Temperament:</b> Peaceful, timid</span></div>
  <p>Tiny, charming and the best true algae eater for a small tank — but <strong>they need a mature, established aquarium</strong> with real biofilm and soft green algae to graze on. Put them in a new tank and they starve. Most are still wild-caught and arrive stressed, so buy the plumpest ones you can see and keep six or more.</p>
  <p class="goes-with"><strong>Good with:</strong> all peaceful community fish, shrimp, snails.</p>
  <p class="avoid"><strong>Avoid:</strong> new tanks, cichlids, anything large enough to eat them.</p>
</div>

<div class="fish-card peaceful">
  <h3>10 · Kuhli loach</h3>
  <div class="latin">Pangio kuhlii</div>
  <div class="fish-spec"><span><b>Size:</b> 3–4"</span><span><b>Min tank:</b> 20 gal</span><span><b>Temp:</b> 75–86°F</span><span><b>Level:</b> Bottom</span><span><b>Temperament:</b> Peaceful, shy</span></div>
  <p>Little eel-like loaches that are entirely harmless and enormously entertaining once settled. Keep <strong>five or more</strong> — in smaller numbers they hide permanently and you''ll never see them. Sand substrate and caves are essential, and a tight lid, because they explore.</p>
  <p class="goes-with"><strong>Good with:</strong> bettas, tetras, rasboras, corydoras, gouramis, angelfish.</p>
  <p class="avoid"><strong>Avoid:</strong> large cichlids, crayfish, bare tanks with no hiding places.</p>
</div>

<div class="fish-card caution">
  <h3>11 · Snail-eating loaches (clown, yoyo, skunk)</h3>
  <div class="latin">Chromobotia / Botia species</div>
  <div class="fish-spec"><span><b>Size:</b> 5–12"</span><span><b>Min tank:</b> 40 gal (clown: 75+)</span><span><b>Temp:</b> 75–86°F</span><span><b>Level:</b> Bottom</span><span><b>Temperament:</b> Boisterous, social</span></div>
  <p>Intelligent, playful and genuinely long-lived — clown loaches can pass 20 years and reach a foot, which the 2-inch juvenile in the shop tank does not suggest. They''re excellent fish for a big tank and a terrible impulse buy. They must be kept in groups of five or more.</p>
  <p class="goes-with"><strong>Good with:</strong> barbs, danios, larger tetras, plecos, gouramis — in a large tank.</p>
  <p class="avoid"><strong>Avoid:</strong> all snails, shrimp, small tanks, being kept singly.</p>
</div>

<h3>Invertebrates</h3>

<div class="fish-card caution">
  <h3>18 · Cherry shrimp (neocaridina)</h3>
  <div class="fish-spec"><span><b>Size:</b> 1–1.5"</span><span><b>Min tank:</b> 5 gal</span><span><b>Temp:</b> 65–78°F</span><span><b>Level:</b> Bottom/plants</span><span><b>Temperament:</b> Peaceful, prey</span></div>
  <p>Fascinating, hardworking and cheap to start a colony with. The compatibility question isn''t whether fish will kill adult shrimp — most won''t — it''s whether <strong>shrimplets survive to adulthood.</strong> In a fish community they usually don''t. A dense carpet of java moss changes the odds dramatically.</p>
  <p><strong>Never use copper-based medications</strong> or many snail treatments: copper is lethal to all shrimp.</p>
  <p class="goes-with"><strong>Good with:</strong> corydoras, otocinclus, bristlenose, snails, small peaceful nano fish.</p>
  <p class="avoid"><strong>Avoid:</strong> angelfish, all cichlids, fin-nippers, crayfish, goldfish, loaches.</p>
</div>

<div class="fish-card peaceful">
  <h3>19 · Amano shrimp</h3>
  <div class="latin">Caridina multidentata</div>
  <div class="fish-spec"><span><b>Size:</b> 2"</span><span><b>Min tank:</b> 10 gal</span><span><b>Temp:</b> 65–80°F</span><span><b>Level:</b> Bottom</span><span><b>Temperament:</b> Peaceful</span></div>
  <p>Bigger and tougher than cherry shrimp, and the best algae-eating invertebrate you can buy — they''ll clear hair algae that nothing else touches. Because of their size they survive in community tanks where cherries would be eaten. They won''t breed in freshwater (larvae need brackish), so the population stays exactly where you put it.</p>
  <p class="goes-with"><strong>Good with:</strong> most peaceful community fish, gouramis, rams, corydoras.</p>
  <p class="avoid"><strong>Avoid:</strong> cichlids, goldfish, crayfish, large predatory fish.</p>
</div>

<div class="fish-card peaceful">
  <h3>20 · Snails (nerite, mystery, ramshorn)</h3>
  <div class="fish-spec"><span><b>Size:</b> 0.5–2"</span><span><b>Min tank:</b> 5 gal</span><span><b>Temp:</b> 65–82°F</span><span><b>Level:</b> All surfaces</span><span><b>Temperament:</b> Peaceful</span></div>
  <p><strong>Nerites</strong> are the best algae eaters and won''t breed in freshwater — they lay harmless white eggs that never hatch. <strong>Mystery snails</strong> are big, personable and only breed above the waterline, so they''re easy to control. <strong>Ramshorns</strong> multiply to match the food available, which makes them either a free clean-up crew or a nuisance depending on how much you overfeed.</p>
  <p>All snails need calcium in the water for shell health, and none of them tolerate copper-based medications.</p>
  <p class="goes-with"><strong>Good with:</strong> nearly all community fish and shrimp.</p>
  <p class="avoid"><strong>Avoid:</strong> snail-eating loaches, pufferfish, assassin snails, most cichlids, crayfish.</p>
</div>

<div class="fish-card expert">
  <h3>21 · Freshwater crabs</h3>
  <div class="fish-spec"><span><b>Size:</b> 1–3"</span><span><b>Setup:</b> Usually paludarium</span><span><b>Temp:</b> 75–82°F</span><span><b>Level:</b> Bottom/land</span><span><b>Temperament:</b> Opportunistic</span></div>
  <p>Read the species carefully before buying. <strong>Red claw crabs need brackish water and dry land</strong> to climb out onto — the most commonly mis-sold invertebrate in the trade. <strong>Vampire crabs</strong> need a planted paludarium, not an aquarium. <strong>Thai micro crabs</strong> are the genuine exception: fully aquatic, thumbnail-sized, and safe with nano fish and shrimp.</p>
  <p>Most crabs are also escape artists and will catch small or sleeping fish given the chance.</p>
  <p class="goes-with"><strong>Good with:</strong> a species-appropriate setup of their own; micro crabs with nano fish.</p>
  <p class="avoid"><strong>Avoid:</strong> standard community tanks, small fish, shrimp, anything they can grab.</p>
</div>

<div class="fish-card expert">
  <h3>22 · Crayfish / "freshwater lobsters"</h3>
  <div class="fish-spec"><span><b>Size:</b> 3–6"</span><span><b>Min tank:</b> 20 gal, species only</span><span><b>Temp:</b> 65–80°F</span><span><b>Level:</b> Bottom</span><span><b>Temperament:</b> Predatory</span></div>
  <p>Genuinely interesting animals with real personality — and completely unsuited to a community tank. They hunt at night, pulling sleeping fish off plants, and they''ll eliminate a shrimp colony and a snail population within weeks. They''re also cannibalistic when moulting, so even keeping two together needs plenty of caves.</p>
  <p class="goes-with"><strong>Good with:</strong> a tank of their own, with a secure lid.</p>
  <p class="avoid"><strong>Avoid:</strong> everything else.</p>
</div>

<h3>Cichlids &amp; coldwater</h3>

<div class="fish-card expert">
  <h3>15 · African cichlids (mbuna)</h3>
  <div class="fish-spec"><span><b>Size:</b> 4–8"</span><span><b>Min tank:</b> 55 gal (75+ better)</span><span><b>Temp:</b> 76–82°F</span><span><b>Level:</b> All</span><span><b>Temperament:</b> Aggressive</span></div>
  <p>Spectacular colours, and effectively their own hobby. They want <strong>hard, alkaline water</strong> (the opposite of what most community fish prefer), a rock-piled tank, and — counter-intuitively — <strong>heavy overstocking</strong>, which spreads aggression so no single fish is targeted. Almost nothing else can live with them; bristlenose plecos and some synodontis catfish are the usual exceptions.</p>
  <p class="avoid"><strong>Avoid:</strong> mixing with any community fish, soft water, understocking, small tanks.</p>
</div>

<div class="fish-card expert">
  <h3>16 · Large American cichlids (oscar, jack dempsey, convict)</h3>
  <div class="fish-spec"><span><b>Size:</b> 6–14"</span><span><b>Min tank:</b> 55–125 gal by species</span><span><b>Temp:</b> 74–82°F</span><span><b>Level:</b> All</span><span><b>Temperament:</b> Aggressive, territorial</span></div>
  <p>Oscars are famously personable — they recognise their owners and can be hand-fed — and famously messy, needing oversized filtration and big weekly water changes. A single oscar needs 75 gallons minimum. Convicts are the small exception at 5–6 inches, but they breed relentlessly and defend their fry against everything in the tank.</p>
  <p class="avoid"><strong>Avoid:</strong> any fish small enough to swallow, shrimp, snails, undersized tanks.</p>
</div>

<div class="fish-card caution">
  <h3>17 · Goldfish</h3>
  <div class="latin">Carassius auratus</div>
  <div class="fish-spec"><span><b>Size:</b> 6–8" fancy, 12"+ common</span><span><b>Min tank:</b> 20–40 gal fancy; pond for commons</span><span><b>Temp:</b> 65–72°F</span><span><b>Level:</b> All</span><span><b>Temperament:</b> Peaceful but messy</span></div>
  <p>Coldwater fish that belong with other goldfish. Don''t mix <strong>fancy</strong> varieties (fantails, orandas, ryukins) with <strong>single-tailed</strong> ones (commons, comets, shubunkins) either — the streamlined single-tails are far faster and outcompete the fancies for food, and commons reach a foot or more and truly need a pond.</p>
  <p class="goes-with"><strong>Good with:</strong> other goldfish of the same body type; white cloud minnows in cooler setups.</p>
  <p class="avoid"><strong>Avoid:</strong> tropical fish, shrimp, small snails, anything needing warm water.</p>
</div>
