<h2>📏 Six Rules That Matter More Than the Chart</h2>
<div class="vet-tip">
  <h4>💚 Get these right and most compatibility problems never appear</h4>
  <p><strong>1. Tank size fixes aggression.</strong> Most "incompatible" pairings are really space problems. The same two fish that fight in a 20 gallon often coexist in a 55 because there''s room to avoid each other.</p>
  <p><strong>2. Buy schooling fish in proper groups.</strong> Six is the minimum, eight to ten is better. Under-schooled fish are stressed fish, and stressed fish nip, hide and get sick. A small group of tiger barbs is far nastier than a large one.</p>
  <p><strong>3. Match temperature ranges, not just temperament.</strong> Two peaceful species that want 68°F and 82°F will never both thrive. Check the overlap before you buy.</p>
  <p><strong>4. Match water hardness too.</strong> Mollies and African cichlids want hard, alkaline water; cardinal tetras, rams and most shrimp prefer soft and acidic. This quietly kills more fish than aggression does.</p>
  <p><strong>5. Fill the levels, don''t crowd one.</strong> A good community uses the whole tank — top swimmers, mid-water schoolers, bottom dwellers. Six species that all occupy mid-water will feel far more crowded than the same number spread across all three.</p>
  <p><strong>6. If it fits in the mouth, it''s food.</strong> Not aggression — just biology. Always compare <em>adult</em> sizes, never the sizes in the shop tank.</p>
</div>

<h2>✅ Four Stocking Plans That Just Work</h2>
<div class="table-wrap">
  <table class="compare">
    <thead><tr><th>Tank</th><th>Stocking</th><th>Why it works</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>10 gallon</b><br>Nano</td><td>1 betta + 6 pygmy corydoras + 2 nerite snails</td><td>Three levels used, no fin temptation, tiny bioload</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>20 gallon long</b><br>First community</td><td>10 harlequin rasbora + 6 corydoras + 1 honey gourami + amano shrimp</td><td>All peaceful, matched temperature, a centrepiece without aggression</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>40 gallon breeder</b><br>Showpiece</td><td>2 angelfish + 12 rummy-nose tetra + 8 corydoras + 1 bristlenose pleco</td><td>Tetras too big for adult angels to eat; footprint suits everyone</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>75 gallon</b><br>Big community</td><td>20 larger tetras + 10 corydoras + 6 kuhli loaches + 2 pearl gourami + 1 bristlenose</td><td>Large schools, full use of levels, forgiving water volume</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<h2>&#128462; Free Printable: The Full Compatibility Chart</h2><p>The whole grid on one page, with the tricky pairings and the six rules underneath. Click it to view full size, or download the original to print and take shopping.</p><figure class="pgp-figure img-full img-center"><img src="/img/fish-compatibility-chart.jpg" class="pgp-img" alt="Freshwater fish compatibility chart: a 22 by 22 grid covering bettas, tetras, fin-nippers, danios, guppies, livebearers, corydoras, plecos, otocinclus, loaches, gouramis, angelfish, dwarf cichlids, African and large American cichlids, goldfish, shrimp, snails, crabs and crayfish"><figcaption>Our printable freshwater compatibility chart &mdash; free to download and share.</figcaption></figure><div class="download-card"><div class="dl-thumb"><img src="/img/fish-compatibility-chart.jpg" alt="Compatibility chart" class="no-zoom"></div><div class="dl-body"><span class="dl-tag">Free download</span><h3>Freshwater Fish Compatibility Chart</h3><p>All 22 species groups, 231 checked pairings, the eight pairings people get wrong, and the six rules that matter more than the chart.</p><a class="dl-btn" href="/download/fish-compatibility-chart.jpg">&#11015;&#65039; Download free</a><span class="dl-note">Free for everyone &mdash; print it, pin it, take it to the shop. No sign-up, no email needed.</span></div></div><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<details class="faq-item"><summary>Can I keep a betta with other fish or not?</summary><p>Often yes — in a 10 gallon or larger, heavily planted, with peaceful bottom dwellers like corydoras and kuhli loaches, or calm mid-water fish like harlequin rasbora and ember tetras. But temperament varies enormously between individual bettas, so always have a divider or spare tank ready. Never with guppies, gouramis or fin-nippers.</p></details>
<details class="faq-item"><summary>Why did my "peaceful" fish suddenly turn aggressive?</summary><p>Usually one of four things: it matured and became territorial, it''s breeding and defending a nest, the group is too small so the pecking order has nowhere to disperse, or the tank is too crowded for anyone to hold territory peacefully. Adding hiding places and breaking up sight lines with plants and decor fixes a surprising amount.</p></details>
<details class="faq-item"><summary>Will my fish eat my shrimp?</summary><p>Adult shrimp survive alongside most peaceful community fish. Shrimplets get eaten by almost anything with a mouth. If you want a breeding colony, either keep shrimp alone or plant heavily with java moss so the babies have somewhere to hide. Never keep shrimp with cichlids, loaches, crayfish or large-mouthed fish.</p></details>
<details class="faq-item"><summary>How many fish can I actually put in my tank?</summary><p>Ignore the inch-per-gallon rule — it''s meaningless. Work from adult sizes, research each species'' minimum tank, add stock slowly in small batches, and let your <strong>nitrate readings</strong> be the judge: if you can''t hold nitrate under 40 ppm with a weekly 25% water change, you''re overstocked regardless of what any rule says.</p></details>
<details class="faq-item"><summary>Can I add new fish to an established tank?</summary><p>Yes, but slowly — a few at a time with two to three weeks between additions, so the bacteria colony can grow to match. Quarantine new arrivals in a separate tank for two to four weeks if you can; almost every disease outbreak in an established aquarium arrives with new fish. Rearranging the decor when you add fish also helps disrupt existing territories.</p></details>
<details class="faq-item"><summary>What''s the most beginner-proof community you can build?</summary><p>A 20 gallon long with harlequin rasbora, corydoras and a honey gourami. Everything in it is hardy, peaceful, wants the same temperature and water, and occupies a different level of the tank. Add a few amano shrimp and nerite snails and you have a genuinely low-drama aquarium.</p></details>

<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
<p>Compatibility comes down to three questions, asked before you buy rather than after: <strong>will it fit in anyone''s mouth when fully grown, does it want the same water and temperature, and does it have enough space and company of its own kind?</strong> Get those right and the chart above will rarely surprise you.</p>
<p>And when a shop assistant tells you a fish "stays small" or "grows to fit the tank" — check for yourself. That single habit will prevent most of the mistakes in this guide.</p>
